course: everyday
lesson: 42
title: Traffic lights, crossings and parked cars
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The safest place to cross is a crossing: drivers expect people there and the crossing gives you a turn to walk.
explain: At a crossing with lights you press the button and wait for the signal to walk: a green or white walking person; a red person or a red or orange hand means do not start crossing.
explain: A zebra crossing has black and white stripes and, in the UK, flashing orange globes on poles; drivers should stop for people waiting there, but you wait until the traffic has really stopped before you step out.
explain: Even when the signal says walk, you still look, because a driver turning the corner or rushing through a red light may not stop.
explain: Parked cars are like a wall: a girl stepping out from between two parked vans cannot see the cars coming, and the drivers cannot see her, because she is shorter than the vans.
explain: A boy in Toronto crossed when the walk signal had just turned red because the cars had not moved yet; a car turning the corner did not see him and hit him.
explain: A girl in Auckland crossed at a zebra crossing without looking because she thought cars must stop; a driver did not see her and braked only just in time.
fact: A crossing is safer than crossing anywhere else, because drivers expect people there.
fact: At light-controlled crossings you press the button and wait for the walk signal.
fact: A red standing person or a red or orange hand means do not start to cross.
fact: In the USA a flashing hand or a countdown means do not start crossing, but finish crossing if you are already in the road.
fact: Even with the walk signal you look both ways, because a car may still be moving or turning.
fact: A zebra crossing has black and white stripes on the road.
fact: In the UK zebra crossings have flashing orange globes on black and white poles, called Belisha beacons.
fact: At a zebra crossing you wait until the traffic has stopped before stepping onto the road.
fact: Crossing between parked cars is dangerous because drivers cannot see you and you cannot see them.
fact: A driver reversing out of a driveway or a parking space may not see a child behind the car.
fact: If you must pass parked cars, stop at their outside edge and look before going on.
fact: A green walking signal does not always mean every driver has seen you.
fact: Many walkers are hit by cars turning a corner across the crossing.
exercise: Why is a crossing a safer place to cross than anywhere else on the road? | Drivers expect people to be crossing there | Cars are not allowed to drive over it at any time | The road is always narrower there | Crossings are always well lit at night
exercise: The walking person on the crossing signal has turned green. What should you do before stepping out? | Check that the cars have really stopped | Run across as fast as you can | Wait for the signal to turn red | Nothing, the green light makes you safe
exercise: What does a steady red hand or a red standing person mean? | Do not start to cross | Cross quickly before the cars come | Cross only if you are in a hurry | The button is broken
exercise: A girl in Chicago is halfway across when the signal starts flashing an orange hand. What should she do? | Keep going calmly to the other side | Turn round and go back | Stop and wait in the middle | Run back to the kerb and press the button again
exercise: What does a zebra crossing look like? | Black and white stripes across the road | A green walking person on a pole | A yellow line along the kerb | A red circle painted on the road
exercise: In the UK, what are the flashing orange globes beside a zebra crossing for? | To show drivers that there is a zebra crossing | To tell walkers to stop and wait until they go dark | To light up the road for cyclists | To show that the road is closed
exercise: At a zebra crossing, when do you step onto the road? | When the traffic has stopped for you | As soon as you reach the stripes | When the first car slows a little | When the beacons stop flashing
exercise: Why do you still look both ways when the walk signal is on? | A car may be turning the corner or running a red light | The signal is often wrong | Cars only stop for people who look | Looking at the cars makes the walk signal last a lot longer
exercise: Why is stepping out between two parked cars dangerous? | Drivers cannot see you and you cannot see them | Parked cars might start to roll | It is a much longer way across the road from there | Parked cars have their lights on
exercise: How can you be seen better if you really must pass parked cars? | Stop at the outer edge of the cars and look before going on | Crouch down low as you cross | Run out quickly from behind them | Walk out backwards so you can watch both ways at the same time
exercise: Why is it dangerous to play behind a parked car in a driveway? | The driver reversing may not see a small child | Parked cars give off harmful fumes that make you ill | Driveways are slippery | The car alarm might go off
exercise: Why does a child see less from between parked vans than an adult does? | The child is shorter than the vans | Children's eyes cannot see as far as grown-ups' eyes | Vans have dark windows | The van mirrors block the view
exercise: A boy wants to cross a busy street. A light-controlled crossing is a short walk away. What is best? | Walk to the crossing and use it | Cross where he is, but look carefully | Cross between parked cars to save time | Wait until a car flashes its lights at him
test: A girl in Melbourne presses the button at the lights. A bus is still coming. What should she do? | Wait until the walk signal shows and the traffic stops | Cross now, because she pressed the button and the bus must stop | Wave to the bus driver and cross | Cross behind the bus as it passes
test: Why might a driver who is turning right at a junction not stop for a boy who is crossing on the walk signal? | The driver may be watching the other traffic and not see him | Turning drivers never have to stop | The walk signal does not count for any cars that are turning a corner | The boy is crossing at the wrong colour
test: Which is true? | A green walk signal means it is your turn, not that every car will stop | A green walk signal means no car can possibly come anywhere near you | A red hand means cross quickly | Zebra crossings are only for bicycles
test: A boy in Manchester waits at a zebra crossing. A car is coming fast and is still far away. What should he do? | Wait to see that it is stopping before stepping out | Step out at once, since drivers must stop | Run across before the car arrives | Turn away from the road so the driver knows he is not crossing
test: A ball rolls into the road between two parked cars. What should a girl do? | Stop, and ask an adult to help get the ball safely | Run after it quickly before a car comes along and squashes it | Step out between the cars to look | Wait for the ball to roll back by itself
test: Why do traffic lights for walkers in many US cities show a countdown? | So you know how long is left and do not start too late | So you can race the cars to the other side before they move off | So drivers know when to speed up | So you know when the bus is coming
test: A family car is backing out of a driveway. Where is the most dangerous place for a small child to stand? | Right behind the car | On the porch by the door | Next to an adult on the lawn | Inside the house by the window
test: Why is the walk signal not a reason to stop looking? | Some drivers make mistakes or do not see the light | The signal changes colour too quickly to be sure of it | Cars drive on the pavement at crossings | Walk signals never work in the rain
test: Which place to cross would a road safety officer choose? | A crossing with lights, after checking the traffic | Between two parked lorries, as it is quick and short | Just round a bend, where it is quiet | Where your friends are already crossing
test: the hand's flashing n i haven't stepped off the kerb yet, go? | No, wait for the next walk signal | Yes, run fast | Yes, flashing means go, just walk quicker | Yes, wave at cars
test: green man, can i just go | Look both ways first, cars may be turning | Yes, never look | Yes, run straight out, the cars all have to stop | Yes, with your eyes closed
test: zebra crossing in leeds, step out straight away? | Wait till the traffic has stopped | Yes, cars always stop at zebras, just go | Yes, run across | Yes, if you wave
test: gonna cut through between the parked cars | No, drivers can't see you there | Yes, it's quicker, everyone does it | Yes, if you're small | Yes, if you run
test: light's green for me but this car's turning without slowing | Wait and let it pass, then cross | Walk out, it is your turn | Step out and wave at him | Run in front of it quickly
test: zebra crossing so cars have to stop, no need to look right? | Always look and wait until cars stop | Yes, you can walk straight out without looking | Yes, if you walk slowly | Yes, unless it is dark
test: red man flashing, still loads of time innit | Do not start, wait for the next green | Run across, there is loads of time left | Cross if you are fast | Cross diagonally to save time
test: heard a boy ran out between parked cars for his ball and a van just missed him, the driver went mental | Real lesson: drivers cannot see you between parked cars, so do not cross there | Real lesson: vans are more dangerous than cars and should be banned | It proves balls should never be played with | It is gossip, so parked cars do not matter at all when you cross
test: my mate says when the green man shows u can just go without looking, the cars have to stop | Not safe; even with the walk signal look both ways, a car may still be turning | He is right, the green man means no cars can come, so you never need to look | He is right, if you walk quickly | He is right, drivers always stop
test: Ava's dad reversed over their neighbour's bin and people say he's a terrible driver, pass it on | Do not pass it on; it is unkind, and reversing drivers cannot see everything | Pass it on, because it proves it | Pass it on, so people avoid his car | Pass it on, and say he hit a cat and a bike too, so everyone is warned
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of a flashing hand or countdown signal in the United States? | A pedestrian must not commence crossing, but may complete a crossing already begun | A pedestrian may commence crossing at once | A pedestrian must stop in the middle of the road and await the next green signal before continuing | A pedestrian must run back to the kerb
exam: Identify the device known in the United Kingdom as a Belisha beacon. | A flashing orange globe upon a black and white pole at a zebra crossing | A red light at a railway crossing | A sign indicating a school ahead | A white light mounted upon a police car to warn traffic of an accident ahead
exam: It being given that a pedestrian waits at a zebra crossing, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To wait until the traffic has stopped before stepping into the road | To step into the road at once, since all vehicles are required by law to stop | To run across before any vehicle arrives | To cross only when a pedestrian light shows
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a vehicle reversing out of a driveway. | The driver may be unable to see a child situated behind the vehicle | The driver is always able to see clearly behind the vehicle by means of the mirrors | Reversing vehicles move too slowly to cause injury | A reversing vehicle always sounds a warning
